% Lines at the same level are read from the top, each with its own
% connective: "A / or B / and C" means (A or B) and C, and the verifier warns
% of it (or_then_and). An "and" that belongs to one alternative only is
% indented under it ("high"); a disjunction that an "and" must cover has its
% alternative indented under its first line ("registered high scorer").
the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    the a of *a person* is *a number*; undefined.
    the b of *a person* is *a number*; undefined.
    *a person* is registered; undefined.
    *a person* is high.
    *a person* is a registered high scorer.

the knowledge base and_or_precedence includes:

a person is high
    if the a of the person is a number H1
    and H1 >= 80
    or the b of the person is a number H2
        and H2 >= 80.

a person is a registered high scorer
    if the a of the person is a number A1
        or the b of the person is a number A1
    and A1 >= 80
    and the person is registered.

scenario s is:
    the a of ann is 90.
    the b of bob is 90.
    the a of cat is 50.
    the b of cat is 85.
    ann is registered.
    cat is registered.
    high expects answers ["ann is high", "bob is high", "cat is high"].
    scorer expects answers ["ann is a registered high scorer", "cat is a registered high scorer"].

query high is:
    which person is high.

query scorer is:
    which person is a registered high scorer.
